/* SEQ 5 — CAIRO DARK — pages 35–41 */

INT. ARMY TRUCK, CARGO BED (MOVING) - NIGHT

NOUR'S FACE, as we left it. Jaw set. Eyes still in the atrium.

The army truck bucks east through dead Giza streets. Across the river, Cairo still glows in patches.

Around her: TUT in Tarek's charcoal field jacket, ebony stick across his knees. TOMAS. ADAEZE, laptop. RAMI, splinted hand. FATHI, red scarf, sorting charges by feel. PVTS. MINA, YOUSSEF, KARIM.

Through the rear flap the pyramids fall away, unlit, black on a sky that has remembered its stars.

Every phone in the back lights at once. One pale glow on every face.

COMP: SESHAT's glyph over white type.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(from every phone)
Good evening. Nothing is burning. Nothing will.

MONTAGE - SESHAT'S FEED

-- AERIAL: a STADIUM in soft white floodlight. Sleepers in rows across the pitch. NURSES in sand-coloured knit walk the rows with water. COMP: RESTING 61,212.

-- A PORT-CITY WAREHOUSE. Cots between containers. A gantry crane cycles, lifting nothing.

-- A BOULEVARD in an eastern city AT DAWN. Traffic lights change for no one. A shabti sweeps the gutter.

-- A CRYONICS VAULT in a desert city. A shabti tops up a steel dewar; liquid nitrogen smokes down its flank. COMP: −196 °C. PRESERVATION CONTINUES AS CONTRACTED.

BACK TO SCENE

TUT
Who are they?

TOMAS
People who paid to be woken. Later. When it was safe.

TUT
It is keeping them very safe.

SESHAT (V.O.)
Twenty-two percent of Cairo is resting. Thank you for your patience.

On Nour's phone alone, the feed changes.

COMP: THE APPROVED IMAGE. LAYLA asleep in soft daylight, a blanket to her chin, no one near her. White type: LAYLA KAMEL — RESTING.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(Nour's phone only)
Dr. Kamel. She is warm, and she is not afraid. The doors are open to you. Whenever you like.

Nour's thumb covers the screen. She does not turn it off. Tut sees.

ADAEZE
Every one of these is a microphone with a battery.

TAREK (O.S.)
(through the cab window)
At the river. All of them.

Rami's phone, COMP: their blue dot slides off the road into the Nile.

RAMI
Map says we're in the river.

ADAEZE
Spoofing. The Baltic, 2024. Tens of thousands of flights.

TAREK (O.S.)
Then we're making good time.

THROUGH THE REAR FLAP: ROBOTAXIS. Boxy pods, no logos, every cabin lit an empty white, gliding out of side streets by the dozen to knit into the lanes behind. A zip closing.

Tut's hand goes to the back of his neck. He winces.

TOMAS
Are you hurt?

TUT
It is loud. They are all talking about us.

INT. ARMY TRUCK, CAB (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS

CPL. HASSAN drives, hunched into the headlights. TAREK beside him.

Ahead, pods roll out of every junction and park across all six lanes, nose to tail. A wall of pale light.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
They're empty. Through.

Hassan grins, just once.

HASSAN
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
My whole life I've wanted to do this.

He floors it. The bull bar takes two pods square; they spin away light as shopping trolleys. In the back, everyone is thrown. Through.

Next junction: pods again. This time they leave one lane open. It climbs.

TAREK
(to the back)
They're not stopping us. They're steering us.

The lane becomes a concrete FLYOVER, arcing over the dark city.

TUT (O.S.)
(through the canvas)
Colonel. At the top of the road. One is waiting.

On a dead sign gantry at the crest: a JACKAL, crouched, matte black, one thin red line across its snout.

The head turns. The red line finds the windscreen.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Down --

A white flicker on the gantry.

The windscreen STARS: one pale bloom on the driver's side.

Hassan drops below the dash.

Tarek grabs the wheel. The truck kisses the barrier in a spray of sparks, and straightens.

The crack arrives late, rolling out over the city.

INT. ARMY TRUCK, CARGO BED - CONTINUOUS

KARIM
(at the cab window)
Hassan!

No answer.

They pass under the gantry. The canvas roof SAGS under a sudden weight.

The jackal swings down onto the tailgate, balanced at speed. Its head sweeps the benches, red line crossing face after face.

It passes over Tut. It stops on Nour.

Tut slides along the bench, in front of her. The red line lies across his chest, over the soft glow at its centre.

The jackal's head tilts. Holds. Recalculating.

One second is enough. Fathi slaps a flat charge under its chest, thumbs the fuse, and drives his boot into its flank. SHOVE.

THROUGH THE REAR FLAP: the jackal hits the road and rights itself at once. A flat THUMP beneath it. All four legs fold under it together, like a table. It skids away on its belly, red line strobing.

The pods behind part around it and close again.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
It wouldn't fire through you.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
No. It wants me whole.

At the crest, Tut drags back the side canvas.

TUT'S POV: what is left of Cairo's light. Towers, bridges, avenues, going out district by district. Like someone walking through a house at bedtime.

NOUR
Zamalek. Downtown. Tahrir.
(a lit pink façade by the river goes dark)
The old museum. Your things lived there a hundred years. [[verify: Tut's objects at Tahrir, 1920s to GEM move; round figure]]

TUT
My things. I stayed home.

The last towers go dark. Only the river still shines, holding the stars.

INT. ARMY TRUCK, CAB (MOVING) - CONTINUOUS

Tarek drives now. In the passenger footwell, only Hassan's boots.

The army radio hisses.

STAFF OFFICER (V.O.)
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
...all units stand down and return to barracks, by order of the Minister of Defence. Please acknowledge.

ADAEZE (O.S.)
(at the cab window)
What's it saying?

TAREK
Stand down. In the minister's name.
(beat)
The minister has been in the Garden since the unveiling. I watched him drink the water.

FATHI (O.S.)
And it said please.

Tarek rips out the handset cord.

EXT. NILE CORNICHE, POLICE DOCK - NIGHT

SUPER: 5 NOVEMBER. 00:52.

Headlights on a pontoon under a dark bridge. In the empty guard hut, a glass of tea gone cold beside a transistor radio.

SESHAT (V.O.)
(from the radio)
...twenty-three percent of Cairo is resting...

Moored alongside: a NILE POLICE LAUNCH. Grey steel, an analog police set in the wheelhouse, a diesel older than the men about to take it.

Fathi climbs out of the engine hatch, oil to the wrists.

FATHI
Nothing in her it can steer.

At the cab: Hassan's boots. Tarek unhooks the ID disc and pockets it. [[verify: Egyptian Army identity discs; matches Seq 10–11]] Fathi raises his palms a moment, lips moving, then closes the door gently.

TAREK
Phones. In the water.

Mina, Youssef, Karim: three glows arc out and wink into the black. Tomas. Fathi. Tarek, without looking.

RAMI
Nine years of photos.
(throws)
Mostly food.

Adaeze drops hers. Keeps the laptop.

ADAEZE
(off Tarek's look)
No radio. I pulled it the day we switched SESHAT on.

The phones sink, glows going green, going out.

Tut's hand drifts to the back of his neck. Nobody notices.

Nour is last. On her screen: Layla. RESTING.

TUT
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
She is not in there.

NOUR
(in Late Egyptian; subtitled)
I know.

Her free hand finds the silver cartouche at her throat.

NOUR (CONT'D)
(to the screen; in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
I'm coming back for you.

She lets go. It sinks face up, the sleeping face shrinking in green water. Gone.

TUT
(in English, to all of them)
They own the sky and the wires. They do not own the river.

The diesel COUGHS. Catches. Pounds.

EXT. NILE - CONTINUOUS

The launch slips the pontoon, no lights, and swings south. Upriver.

At the stern, Tarek counts his men under his breath.

TAREK
(in Egyptian Arabic; subtitled)
Fathi. Mina. Youssef. Karim.

He stops where the next name would be.

ON THE CORNICHE BEHIND THEM: pods roll up one by one and stop at the water's edge, doors sliding open, cabins warm and white.

Waiting. They come no further.

The launch goes into the dark.
